ren":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex gap-2","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"w-36 shrink-0 text-faint","children":"Outlook it expresses"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"text-text","children":"moderately-bullish"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"flex gap-2","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"w-36 shrink-0 text-faint","children":"Opens with"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"text-text","children":"a debit"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"flex gap-2","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"w-36 shrink-0 text-faint","children":"Best case"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"text-text","children":"Spread width minus net debit"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"flex gap-2","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"w-36 shrink-0 text-faint","children":"Worst case"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"text-text","children":"Net debit paid"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"flex gap-2","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"w-36 shrink-0 text-faint","children":"Breakeven"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"text-text","children":"Lower strike plus net debit"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"flex gap-2","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"w-36 shrink-0 text-faint","children":"Exposures"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"text-text","children":"delta positive · gamma mixed · theta mixed · vega negative"}]]}]]}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"rounded-card border px-[18px] py-4 transition-colors duration-panel ease-standard bg-card border-border shadow-card","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-3 flex items-center gap-2","children":["$undefined",["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-base font-semibold leading-tight","children":["Lesson",false]}],false]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"text-[12.5px] leading-relaxed text-muted [&_h1]:mb-2 [&_h1]:text-base [&_h1]:font-bold [&_h1]:text-text [&_h2]:mb-1.5 [&_h2]:mt-5 [&_h2]:text-[13px] [&_h2]:font-bold [&_h2]:text-text [&_h3]:mb-1 [&_h3]:mt-4 [&_h3]:text-[12px] [&_h3]:font-semibold [&_h3]:text-text [&_p]:mb-2.5 [&_strong]:font-semibold [&_strong]:text-text [&_ul]:mb-2.5 [&_ul]:list-disc [&_ul]:pl-5 [&_ol]:mb-2.5 [&_ol]:list-decimal [&_ol]:pl-5 [&_li]:mb-1 [&_code]:rounded [&_code]:bg-bg [&_code]:px-1 [&_code]:py-0.5 [&_code]:font-mono [&_code]:text-[11px] [&_code]:text-text [&_table]:mb-3 [&_table]:w-full [&_table]:text-left [&_table]:text-[11.5px] [&_th]:border-b [&_th]:border-border [&_th]:pb-1 [&_th]:pr-3 [&_th]:font-semibold [&_th]:text-text [&_td]:border-b [&_td]:border-border [&_td]:py-1 [&_td]:pr-3 [&_td]:align-top [&_a]:text-teal hover:[&_a]:underline [&_blockquote]:border-l-2 [&_blockquote]:border-border2 [&_blockquote]:pl-3 [&_blockquote]:italic","children":[["$","h1","h1-0",{"children":"Bull Call Spread"}],"\n",["$","p","p-0",{"children":"Two legs in the same expiry: a bought call and a sold call at a higher strike.\r\nAlso called a call debit spread."}],"\n",["$","h2","h2-0",{"children":"The structure"}],"\n",["$","p","p-1",{"children":"The lower strike is the long leg, the higher strike the short leg, same expiry. The net cost is the\r\ndifference between the two premiums — always a debit, since the lower strike is\r\nalways worth more."}],"\n",["$","p","p-2",{"children":"The trade being made is explicit: give up everything above the short strike, in\r\nexchange for paying materially less at entry. A long call breaks even only\r\nafter covering the full premium; a bull call spread breaks even after covering\r\na reduced one."}],"\n",["$","h2","h2-1",{"children":"What it is exposed to"}],"\n",["$","p","p-3",{"children":[["$","strong","strong-0",{"children":"Delta"}]," is positive but smaller than a long call's, because the short leg's\r\nnegative delta partially offsets the long leg's."]}],"\n",["$","p","p-4",{"children":[["$","strong","strong-0",{"children":"Gamma"}]," is mixed and changes sign depending on where the underlying sits. Near\r\nthe long strike the position is gamma-positive; near the short strike it is\r\ngamma-negative. Between them the two roughly offset."]}],"\n",["$","p","p-5",{"children":[["$","strong","strong-0",{"children":"Theta"}]," is mixed for the same reason, and this is the property that makes the\r\nspread behave differently from a long call in a flat market. A long call bleeds\r\nevery day. A bull call spread with the underlying between the strikes bleeds far\r\nless, because the short leg is decaying in the position's favour at the same\r\ntime the long leg decays against it. If the underlying sits above the short\r\nstrike, time decay actually works ",["$","em","em-0",{"children":"for"}]," the position, since it is the short leg\r\nthat has extrinsic value left to lose."]}],"\n",["$","p","p-6",{"children":[["$","strong","strong-0",{"children":"Vega"}]," is net negative when the spread is established with the underlying\r\nbelow the long strike, and the magnitude is small. The spread is substantially\r\nless volatility-sensitive than a long call — a volatility crush that would\r\ndestroy a naked long call leaves a spread comparatively intact, because both\r\nlegs reprice together."]}],"\n",["$","p","p-7",{"children":"That volatility insensitivity is the main structural argument for the spread\r\nover the outright call around scheduled events."}],"\n",["$","h2","h2-2",{"children":"Payoff"}],"\n",["$","ul","ul-0",{"children":["\n",["$","li","li-0",{"children":"Below the lower strike at expiry: both legs expire worthless, the net debit is lost."}],"\n",["$","li","li-1",{"children":"Between the strikes: the long call has intrinsic value, the short does not."}],"\n",["$","li","li-2",{"children":"At or above the upper strike: both are in the money, the difference is fixed at the spread width."}],"\n"]}],"\n",["$","p","p-8",{"children":"Maximum profit is spread width minus net debit. Maximum loss is the net debit.\r\nBreakeven is the lower strike plus the net debit."}],"\n",["$","p","p-9",{"children":"The ratio of these two numbers is fixed by the strikes chosen and is worth\r\ncomputing before entry rather than after. A spread paid for at 40% of its width\r\nrisks 40 to make 60."}],"\n",["$","h2","h2-3",{"children":"Indian market specifics"}],"\n",["$","p","p-10",{"children":[["$","strong","strong-0",{"children":"Strike step matters."}]," NIFTY strikes are 50 apart, BANKNIFTY 100. The\r\n",["$","code","code-0",{"children":"ATM+2"}]," in this lesson's frontmatter means two strike steps, which resolves to a\r\ndifferent width per instrument — the Apply feature substitutes the correct step\r\nfrom the live scrip master rather than assuming one."]}],"\n",["$","p","p-11",{"children":[["$","strong","strong-0",{"children":"Margin benefit."}]," A vertical spread is recognised as a hedged position and\r\nattracts far less margin than the short leg alone. This is the practical reason\r\nspreads dominate retail option selling in India. Note the withdrawal of\r\ncalendar-spread benefit on expiry day from February 2025 applies to spreads\r\nacross ",["$","em","em-0",{"children":"different"}]," expiries, not to a same-expiry vertical like this one."]}],"\n",["$","p","p-12",{"children":[["$","strong","strong-0",{"children":"Costs bite twice."}]," Two legs in, two legs out: four brokerage charges and STT\r\non both sold legs. On a narrow spread in a liquid weekly contract, costs can be\r\na meaningful fraction of maximum profit. The narrower the spread, the worse\r\nthis ratio."]}],"\n",["$","p","p-13",{"children":[["$","strong","strong-0",{"children":"Expiry-day assignment."}]," If the underlying settles between the strikes on a\r\n",["$","em","em-0",{"children":"stock"}]," spread, the long leg is exercised and the short is not, producing a\r\nphysical delivery obligation on one leg only. Index spreads settle in cash and\r\ncarry no such asymmetry."]}],"\n",["$","h2","h2-4",{"children":"How it typically fails"}],"\n",["$","p","p-14",{"children":"The spread rarely produces a catastrophic loss, which is its purpose. It fails\r\nby being chosen for the wrong reason: when a large directional move is what was\r\nexpected, the capped payoff forfeits most of the gain, and the position would\r\nhave been better expressed as an outright call. It also fails quietly on cost —\r\na spread entered too narrow, in an illiquid stock contract with a wide bid-ask,\r\ncan have negative expectancy before the underlying does anything at all."}],"\n",["$","h2","h2-5",{"children":"Related"}],"\n",["$","ul","ul-1",{"children":["\n",["$","li","li-0",{"children":[["$","code","code-0",{"children":"strategies/directional/long-call.md"}]," — uncapped version, higher cost, more vega"]}],"\n",["$","li","li-1",{"children":[["$","code","code-0",{"children":"strategies/vertical-spreads/bull-put-spread.md"}]," — same directional view expressed for a credit"]}],"\n",["$","li","li-2",{"children":[["$","code","code-0",{"children":"foundations/payoff-diagrams.md"}]," — reading the kinked line"]}],"\n"]}]]}]]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[10px] leading-relaxed text-faint","children":["Educational material describing how a structure behaves. Not a recommendation, not a signal, and not personalised advice. Source: ",["$","code",null,{"className":"font-mono","children":"docs/learning/strategies/vertical-spreads/bull-call-spread.md"}]]}]]}],null],null],null]},[null,["$","$L3",null,{"parallelRouterKey":"children","segmentPath":["children","(workspace)","children","learning","children","strategy","children","$4","children"],"error":"$undefined","errorStyles":"$undefined","errorScripts":"$undefined","template":["$","$L5",null,{}],"templateStyles":"$undefined","templateScripts":"$undefined","notFound":"$undefined","notFoundStyles":"$undefined"}]],null]},[null,["$","$L3",null,{"parallelRouterKey":"children","segmentPath":["children","(workspace)","children","learning","children","strategy","children"],"error":"$undefined","errorStyles":"$undefined","errorScripts":"$undefined","template":["$","$L5",null,{}],"templateStyles":"$undefined","templateScripts":"$undefined","notFound":"$und